<!--
  NetForensiq → Wazuh decoders.

  Install to  /var/ossec/etc/decoders/netforensiq_decoders.xml
  then         /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-control restart

  What this is for
  ================
  NetForensiq already emits RFC 5424 syslog carrying a CEF record, and any
  syslog-receiving SIEM can take delivery of it today. Receiving is not the
  same as understanding: without a decoder the whole line lands in `full_log`
  as one string, so an analyst can grep it and cannot alert on it, group by
  it, or count it. These two files are the difference between an export
  format and an integration.

  What it decodes, and what it deliberately leaves alone
  =====================================================
  The CEF **header** is fixed-arity and always present, so it is decoded in
  full: vendor, product, version, the rule identifier, the title, and the CEF
  severity. `cs1` — our own severity word — is decoded too, because the rules
  file keys Wazuh's alert level off it.

  The CEF **extensions** are not all decoded, and that is deliberate rather
  than unfinished. They are conditional: `cs3` appears only when the finding
  maps to an ATT&CK technique, and `src`/`dst`/`proto` only when there is a
  flow behind it. A single regex with optional groups misaligns Wazuh's
  <order> list the moment one of them is absent, and a decoder that silently
  puts the destination address into the protocol field is worse than one that
  does not try. The full CEF line stays in `full_log`, which remains
  searchable, and the fields that matter for alerting are the ones decoded.
-->

<decoder name="netforensiq">
  <program_name>netforensiq</program_name>
</decoder>

<decoder name="netforensiq-cef">
  <parent>netforensiq</parent>
  <!--
    CEF:Version|Vendor|Product|DevVersion|SignatureID|Name|Severity|Extensions

    `(?:[^|\\]|\\.)*` rather than `[^|]*`: the CEF specification escapes a
    literal pipe inside a header field as `\|`, so a field is "anything that
    is not a pipe or a backslash, or a backslash followed by anything". A
    naive [^|]* stops at the escaped pipe and shifts every later field by one.
  -->
  <regex type="pcre2">CEF:\d+\|((?:[^|\\]|\\.)*)\|((?:[^|\\]|\\.)*)\|((?:[^|\\]|\\.)*)\|((?:[^|\\]|\\.)*)\|((?:[^|\\]|\\.)*)\|(\d+)\|.*?\bcs1=(\w+)</regex>
  <order>nf_vendor, nf_product, nf_version, nf_rule, nf_title, nf_cef_severity, nf_severity</order>
</decoder>
